  "subtitles": [
    {
      "id": "passage-01-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 0,
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 6.916666666666667,
      "text": "The Map of Observing the Mysteries of the Heaven and Earth (Liangyi xuanlan tu 兩儀玄覽圖 ),"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 0,
      "start": 6.916666666666667,
      "end": 13.375,
      "text": "which we call here the Xuantu is a worldmap in eight panels created in"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-02-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 0,
      "start": 13.375,
      "end": 18.25,
      "text": "1603 by the Ming dynasty military official Li Yingshi李應試."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-02-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 0,
      "start": 18.25,
      "end": 24.041666666666668,
      "text": "Figure 1: Map of Observing the Mysteries of the Heaven and the Earth."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-02-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 0,
      "start": 24.041666666666668,
      "end": 29.916666666666668,
      "text": "Liaoning Provincial Museum辽宁省博物馆, Shenyang, People's Republic of China"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-03-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 1,
      "start": 29.916666666666668,
      "end": 37.0,
      "text": "There is strong reason to believe that the Manchus obtained this particular map before"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-03-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 1,
      "start": 37.0,
      "end": 43.916666666666664,
      "text": "they conquered China, a surprising development that makes us reconsider the Manchus'"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-03-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 1,
      "start": 43.916666666666664,
      "end": 50.916666666666664,
      "text": "relationship to Western cartography. This interactive websiste will tell the story of"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-01-03-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 1,
      "start": 50.916666666666664,
      "end": 58.708333333333336,
      "text": "this maps and presents some of the evidence linking the Xuantu map to the pre- conquest Manchus"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 58.708333333333336,
      "end": 65.25,
      "text": "Matteo Ricci and the scholar Li Zhizao 李之藻 created in 1602 a map named Complete"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 65.25,
      "end": 71.125,
      "text": "Map of the Myriad Kingdoms of the World ( Kunyu Wanguo Quantu 坤輿萬國全圖)."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 71.125,
      "end": 78.20833333333333,
      "text": "It included geographical descriptions of the historical five continents, complete with"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 78.20833333333333,
      "end": 85.375,
      "text": "place-names, a précis of Ptolemaic cosmology, and paper instruments for measuring polar"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 85.375,
      "end": 92.25,
      "text": "altitude and latitude. Figure 2: Map of myriad countries of the Earth, Kunyu wanguo"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 92.25,
      "end": 97.33333333333333,
      "text": "quantu 坤輿萬國全圖, by Matteo Ricci, Li Zhizao 李之藻, et al., 1602."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-02-01-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 2,
      "start": 97.33333333333333,
      "end": 101.58333333333333,
      "text": "James Ford Bell Library, University of Minnesota."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 101.58333333333333,
      "end": 108.45833333333333,
      "text": "The Xuantu is a large object, measuring 2 meters (78.74 inches) vertically and 4.42"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 108.45833333333333,
      "end": 115.375,
      "text": "meters (174.01 inches) horizontally. As such, it fits awkwardly among Chinese prints"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 115.375,
      "end": 122.25,
      "text": "from the Ming dynasty. Maps of this size meant for a high-end audience of officials"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 122.25,
      "end": 129.5,
      "text": "were rarely prints. Instead, just as for paintings, Chinese artisans normally used silk,"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 129.5,
      "end": 133.5,
      "text": "which was favored for its superior durability."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 133.5,
      "end": 140.375,
      "text": "Silk, however, because of its cost, was more appropriate for the creation of unique"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-03-01-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 3,
      "start": 140.375,
      "end": 144.08333333333334,
      "text": "items, such as decorative folding screens."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 144.08333333333334,
      "end": 151.25,
      "text": "For Sino-Western world maps, the desire to quickly disseminate many copies trumped that"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 151.25,
      "end": 158.25,
      "text": "of creating solid, durable objects. World maps by Matteo Ricci and other Jesuits thus"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 158.25,
      "end": 161.70833333333334,
      "text": "came to occupy an important niche."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 161.70833333333334,
      "end": 168.16666666666666,
      "text": "They were as large in size as silk maps made for important people, yet easily"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 168.16666666666666,
      "end": 175.08333333333334,
      "text": "reproducible and affordable. This was at the root of their success in the late Ming."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 175.08333333333334,
      "end": 179.54166666666666,
      "text": "It is also the reason why so few copies survived:"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 179.54166666666666,
      "end": 186.45833333333334,
      "text": "large prints were fragile. Folding screen with figures in a landscape, Qing dynasty,"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-04-01-subtitle-08",
      "passage": 4,
      "start": 186.45833333333334,
      "end": 189.91666666666666,
      "text": "Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 5,
      "start": 189.91666666666666,
      "end": 196.375,
      "text": "Indeed, Xuantu is important not so much for what it has in it but for"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 5,
      "start": 196.375,
      "end": 203.83333333333334,
      "text": "what it has on it: the repairs, modifications, and annotations added to the original print."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 6,
      "start": 203.83333333333334,
      "end": 211.0,
      "text": "Material alterations, the content of annotations, and the form of the Manchu script are"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 6,
      "start": 211.0,
      "end": 218.16666666666666,
      "text": "three aspects that demonstrate the epistemological engagement of the Manchu translators"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-02-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 6,
      "start": 218.16666666666666,
      "end": 225.41666666666666,
      "text": "of the Later Jin state with the Map of Observing the Mysteries of the Heaven and Earth."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 225.41666666666666,
      "end": 232.5,
      "text": "The Map of Observing Mysteries, like its 1602 predecessor, the Map of Myriad Kingdoms,"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 232.5,
      "end": 239.5,
      "text": "was intended by Ricci to be colored by hand, to better distinguish the continents and"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 239.5,
      "end": 242.95833333333334,
      "text": "land masses from the bodies of water."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 242.95833333333334,
      "end": 249.75,
      "text": "Since the coloring process had to be done after the printing and involved the work"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 249.75,
      "end": 256.625,
      "text": "of another specialized artisan, incurring additional expenses, many copies remained"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 256.625,
      "end": 262.25,
      "text": "black-and-white prints. The Shenyang copy was originally uncolored."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 262.25,
      "end": 269.4166666666667,
      "text": "Yet during its life in the Shenyang palace, an artisan was tasked with embellishing the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-08",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 269.4166666666667,
      "end": 275.6666666666667,
      "text": "mountain ranges across all the continents in the style of Chinese painting."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-09",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 275.6666666666667,
      "end": 282.9166666666667,
      "text": "The color scheme of these decorations—blue, green, and gold—fits the one used throughout"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-03-subtitle-10",
      "passage": 7,
      "start": 282.9166666666667,
      "end": 286.375,
      "text": "the Shenyang palace."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 286.375,
      "end": 293.1666666666667,
      "text": "Besides the repairs and added decorative elements, the Shenyang copy of the Map of"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 293.1666666666667,
      "end": 298.25,
      "text": "Observing Mysteries displays a number of Manchu annotations."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 298.25,
      "end": 304.0416666666667,
      "text": "These inscriptions consisted of both topology and technical elements."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 304.0416666666667,
      "end": 311.2083333333333,
      "text": "The technical elements included the cosmological theory explained in the map's prefaces"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 311.2083333333333,
      "end": 318.2083333333333,
      "text": "and surrounding texts. The theory of the map expounded in the preface by Matteo Ricci"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 318.2083333333333,
      "end": 325.0,
      "text": "presented relationships between Earth's spherical shape, its position in the solar"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 325.0,
      "end": 328.6666666666667,
      "text": "system, and the cycles of night and day."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-05-04-subtitle-08",
      "passage": 8,
      "start": 328.6666666666667,
      "end": 336.0,
      "text": "All of these features, represented by lines on the map, had Manchu translations attached."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-06-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 9,
      "start": 336.0,
      "end": 343.0833333333333,
      "text": "The central text of the map summarized the cosmology in a commentary written in Manchu"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-06-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 9,
      "start": 343.0833333333333,
      "end": 347.5416666666667,
      "text": "placed on the map near the line of the equator."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-06-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 9,
      "start": 347.5416666666667,
      "end": 354.4166666666667,
      "text": "The main divisions of Earth into continents and oceans, described in the preface by"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-06-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 9,
      "start": 354.4166666666667,
      "end": 359.125,
      "text": "Ricci and other texts on the map, were also translated."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-07-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 10,
      "start": 359.125,
      "end": 366.2083333333333,
      "text": "Taken together, they suggest that the Manchu translator had a grasp of and an interest"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-07-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 10,
      "start": 366.2083333333333,
      "end": 374.125,
      "text": "in the “theory” of the map, having read and understood the prefaces and other accompanying texts."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 374.125,
      "end": 380.5833333333333,
      "text": "There are three things that make us believe that this map stood out at its"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 380.5833333333333,
      "end": 387.2083333333333,
      "text": "time. It was repaired, intentionally colored, and had inscriptions of the Manchu"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 387.2083333333333,
      "end": 394.0,
      "text": "transcript on it. When it was used in the Shenyang palace, the Xuantu was damaged."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 394.0,
      "end": 399.375,
      "text": "Winters in Shenyang are bitterly cold, and rooms must be heated."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 399.375,
      "end": 406.625,
      "text": "For this reason, there was a temperature difference between the upper and lower parts of"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 406.625,
      "end": 413.4166666666667,
      "text": "the room. This created condensation, and because the map was most likely displayed"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-01-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 11,
      "start": 413.4166666666667,
      "end": 419.0416666666667,
      "text": "vertically, the dampness damaged the portions closer to the ground."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 12,
      "start": 419.0416666666667,
      "end": 425.5,
      "text": "The bottom corner of the eight roll (left side of the map) of the Shengyang"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 12,
      "start": 425.5,
      "end": 428.9583333333333,
      "text": "copy of the Map of observing mysteries."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-02-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 12,
      "start": 428.9583333333333,
      "end": 435.5833333333333,
      "text": "This area, representing the Southern Hemisphere, was damaged and later repaired."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-02-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 12,
      "start": 435.5833333333333,
      "end": 442.5,
      "text": "An artisan had applied paper paste and, after it dried, redrew the meridians to give"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-02-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 12,
      "start": 442.5,
      "end": 449.6666666666667,
      "text": "the impression of continuity. Further damage to the map reveals the discrepancy between"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-08-02-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 12,
      "start": 449.6666666666667,
      "end": 453.125,
      "text": "the two layers."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 453.125,
      "end": 460.0416666666667,
      "text": "The amount of information included in the reconstruction on the top stratum differed"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 460.0416666666667,
      "end": 463.5,
      "text": "from that of the layer below."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 463.5,
      "end": 468.2916666666667,
      "text": "This was due perhaps to the artisan's limited knowledge."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 468.2916666666667,
      "end": 474.375,
      "text": "But it might also be a reflection of the interests of the map owners."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 474.375,
      "end": 481.625,
      "text": "he Southern Hemisphere on the left side of the map (roll one), for instance, disappeared"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 481.625,
      "end": 485.7916666666667,
      "text": "completely, being covered up in the restoration;"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 485.7916666666667,
      "end": 492.7916666666667,
      "text": "only a circle and some of the radiating meridians were copied on top for consistency."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-08",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 492.7916666666667,
      "end": 499.9583333333333,
      "text": "On the other hand, the northern hemisphere was preserved, and annotations were added to"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-09-01-subtitle-09",
      "passage": 13,
      "start": 499.9583333333333,
      "end": 505.3333333333333,
      "text": "it because it encompassed places the Manchus were interested in."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 14,
      "start": 505.3333333333333,
      "end": 511.9583333333333,
      "text": "Most translations of place-names are concentrated in East Asia, radiating toward"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 14,
      "start": 511.9583333333333,
      "end": 518.9583333333334,
      "text": "Europe.The Manchus, it seems, were primarily preoccupied with locating themselves and"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 14,
      "start": 518.9583333333334,
      "end": 525.6666666666666,
      "text": "their immediate surroundings; in terms of their outward interest, they focused on"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 14,
      "start": 525.6666666666666,
      "end": 532.9166666666666,
      "text": "looking westward. The Manchu homeland is marked out by the translation of three adjacent"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 14,
      "start": 532.9166666666666,
      "end": 539.8333333333334,
      "text": "place-names northeastof the Great Wall: Jurchen (Nüzhen女真), Mount Changbai (Changbai"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 14,
      "start": 539.8333333333334,
      "end": 544.9166666666666,
      "text": "Shan長白山), and the City of the Five Nations (Wuguo Cheng五國城)."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 15,
      "start": 544.9166666666666,
      "end": 551.5416666666666,
      "text": "The translation of Mount Changbai (Changbai Shan長白山), on the other hand, is more"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 15,
      "start": 551.5416666666666,
      "end": 558.3333333333334,
      "text": "revealing of the Manchus' own emerging sense of identity in the sixteenth century."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 15,
      "start": 558.3333333333334,
      "end": 565.4166666666666,
      "text": "The mountain, appearing here in translation as Sanggiyan Alin, was part of an imperial"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 15,
      "start": 565.4166666666666,
      "end": 572.2083333333334,
      "text": "cult throughout the Qing, revered as the birthplace of the mythical founder of the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 15,
      "start": 572.2083333333334,
      "end": 579.2083333333334,
      "text": "dynasty, Bukūri Yongšon. The addition of an in-scription alongside the mountain is an"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-02-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 15,
      "start": 579.2083333333334,
      "end": 586.0833333333334,
      "text": "indication that the place was meaningful for the Manchus as early as the Later Jin."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-03-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 16,
      "start": 586.0833333333334,
      "end": 593.1666666666666,
      "text": "Moving beyond this sphere, immediately neighboring countries such as Japan, Korea, and"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-03-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 16,
      "start": 593.1666666666666,
      "end": 597.9583333333334,
      "text": "the Great Ming are labeled using names of Manchu origin:"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-03-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 16,
      "start": 597.9583333333334,
      "end": 604.8333333333334,
      "text": "Ose, Sohlo, and Nikan. These names do not describe places or countries but, rather,"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-03-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 16,
      "start": 604.8333333333334,
      "end": 611.8333333333334,
      "text": "ethnicities.They were well established in the common geographic knowledge embedded in"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-03-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 16,
      "start": 611.8333333333334,
      "end": 617.7083333333334,
      "text": "the Manchu language, as the Manchus had direct interactions with them."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 617.7083333333334,
      "end": 624.9583333333334,
      "text": "The rest of the translations of place-names on the map are transliterations in Manchu of"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 624.9583333333334,
      "end": 631.9583333333334,
      "text": "the pronunciation of Chinese characters. These include the place-names for India, the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 631.9583333333334,
      "end": 638.9583333333334,
      "text": "Philippines, Java, Madagascar, the Cape of Good Hope, the Fortunate (Canary) Islands,"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 638.9583333333334,
      "end": 646.2083333333334,
      "text": "Greenland,and Novaya Zemlya. The selection of these places cannot be easily explained by"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 646.2083333333334,
      "end": 649.6666666666666,
      "text": "what we readily know about the Manchus."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 649.6666666666666,
      "end": 656.375,
      "text": "However, since they were singled out among all the other locations on the map, we"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 656.375,
      "end": 663.5416666666666,
      "text": "must assume that they triggered a sense of relevance and recognition for the translator"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-10-04-subtitle-08",
      "passage": 17,
      "start": 663.5416666666666,
      "end": 667.0,
      "text": "and for the map's viewership."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 18,
      "start": 667.0,
      "end": 673.375,
      "text": "The translations of places like the Fortunate Islands, the Cape of Good Hope,"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 18,
      "start": 673.375,
      "end": 680.4583333333334,
      "text": "Madagascar, Java, and the Philippines resulted from an awareness of the centers of the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 18,
      "start": 680.4583333333334,
      "end": 687.4583333333334,
      "text": "European trade network around Eurasia. These were, after all, places from which ships"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 18,
      "start": 687.4583333333334,
      "end": 691.625,
      "text": "dropping anchor at Macao would have been coming."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 19,
      "start": 691.625,
      "end": 696.5,
      "text": "However, the form of the inscriptions is also meaningful."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-11-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 19,
      "start": 696.5,
      "end": 703.375,
      "text": "They can help answer questions about the dating and authorship of the translations."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 703.375,
      "end": 710.25,
      "text": "According to the Manchu Veritable Records, the Manchu written language was a direct"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 710.25,
      "end": 716.875,
      "text": "invention of bilingual scholars working for Nurhaci and was established in 1599."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 716.875,
      "end": 723.9583333333334,
      "text": "A well-known passage describes how Nurhaci noticed that Mongolian and Chinese could be"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 723.9583333333334,
      "end": 731.0416666666666,
      "text": "both written and spoken, while the Manchus needed to use Mongolian—a language they did"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 731.0416666666666,
      "end": 735.9166666666666,
      "text": "not speak as natives—to write documentsand proclamations."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 735.9166666666666,
      "end": 743.1666666666666,
      "text": "Consequently, Nurhaci asked the polyglot scholar Erdeni and the judge Gagai to solve the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 743.1666666666666,
      "end": 748.875,
      "text": "problem and modify the Mongolian script so as to write Manchu words."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-08",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 748.875,
      "end": 756.0416666666666,
      "text": "But because Mongolian letters could not perfectly represent Manchu sounds, this version"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-09",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 756.0416666666666,
      "end": 759.5,
      "text": "of the scripthad a few drawbacks."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-10",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 759.5,
      "end": 765.9583333333334,
      "text": "The script could not clearly distinguish a and e,o and u, or k,g, and h."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-11",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 765.9583333333334,
      "end": 772.4166666666666,
      "text": "There was also no distinction between front and back d and t, and there were"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-01-subtitle-12",
      "passage": 20,
      "start": 772.4166666666666,
      "end": 775.9583333333334,
      "text": "uncertainties in spelling certain words."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 775.9583333333334,
      "end": 783.2083333333334,
      "text": "A reform of the language that would address these issues was implemented during the time"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 783.2083333333334,
      "end": 786.6666666666666,
      "text": "of the second Khan, Hong Taiji."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 786.6666666666666,
      "end": 793.375,
      "text": "The most important and visible aspect of this reform was the addition of dots and"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 793.375,
      "end": 797.25,
      "text": "circles to solve the problem of distinction."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 797.25,
      "end": 803.7083333333334,
      "text": "The addition of dots could separate a from e,o from u, and k from g,"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 803.7083333333334,
      "end": 807.3333333333334,
      "text": "while circles would distinguish k from h."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 807.3333333333334,
      "end": 814.125,
      "text": "This way of writing Manchu achieved virtually universal usage and has remained the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-02-subtitle-08",
      "passage": 21,
      "start": 814.125,
      "end": 817.7916666666666,
      "text": "standard way of writing it to this day."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 22,
      "start": 817.7916666666666,
      "end": 824.25,
      "text": "Analysis of the form of the script on the map thus indicates that the Map"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 22,
      "start": 824.25,
      "end": 831.25,
      "text": "of Observing Mysterieswas translated between roughly 1625 and 1636, when the reformed"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 22,
      "start": 831.25,
      "end": 834.9583333333334,
      "text": "version of the Old Manchu script was used."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 22,
      "start": 834.9583333333334,
      "end": 841.0416666666666,
      "text": "In any case, the map's annotations indicate an interest in, as well as an"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 22,
      "start": 841.0416666666666,
      "end": 847.9583333333334,
      "text": "epistemological engagement with, a Sino-Western artifact in a crucial moment, as the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-12-03-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 22,
      "start": 847.9583333333334,
      "end": 852.6666666666666,
      "text": "Manchus were beginning to plan their conquest of China."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 23,
      "start": 852.6666666666666,
      "end": 859.4583333333334,
      "text": "Figure 4 Synthetic map of the Great Ming, Da Ming hunyi tu大明混一圖, original in First"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 23,
      "start": 859.4583333333334,
      "end": 866.625,
      "text": "Historical Archives, Beijing, reprint in Zhongguo Gudai Ditu Ji中国古代地图集 (Beijing, 1990)."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 23,
      "start": 866.625,
      "end": 871.3333333333334,
      "text": "Facsimile from the Shanghai Museum of Navigation 航海博物馆."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 23,
      "start": 871.3333333333334,
      "end": 877.875,
      "text": "Photograph by the author. This section of the map of the Great Ming depicts the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 23,
      "start": 877.875,
      "end": 885.0416666666666,
      "text": "western portion of Korea and displays the Manchu place-name transliterations on affixed"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-06",
      "passage": 23,
      "start": 885.0416666666666,
      "end": 891.9166666666666,
      "text": "labels. In some places, over time, the labels have detached, revealing the original"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-13-01-subtitle-07",
      "passage": 23,
      "start": 891.9166666666666,
      "end": 895.375,
      "text": "Chinese text underneath."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-01-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 24,
      "start": 895.375,
      "end": 902.0833333333334,
      "text": "Repairs, modifications, as well as annotations therefore, all belonged to a broad"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-01-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 24,
      "start": 902.0833333333334,
      "end": 909.0,
      "text": "strategy of translation of cartographic artifacts first practiced in the preconquest"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-01-subtitle-03",
      "passage": 24,
      "start": 909.0,
      "end": 915.4583333333334,
      "text": "period on the map by Li Yingshi and Ricci, then on the Map of the"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-01-subtitle-04",
      "passage": 24,
      "start": 915.4583333333334,
      "end": 920.7916666666666,
      "text": "nine borders, and ultimately, after the conquest, on Ming maps."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-01-subtitle-05",
      "passage": 24,
      "start": 920.7916666666666,
      "end": 927.125,
      "text": "This demonstrates the continuity in translation across the Ming-Qing divide."
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-02-subtitle-01",
      "passage": 25,
      "start": 927.125,
      "end": 934.2083333333334,
      "text": "The presence of a map co-authored by Matteo Ricci in the Shenyang palace is ultimately"
    },
    {
      "id": "passage-14-02-subtitle-02",
      "passage": 25,
      "start": 934.2083333333334,
      "end": 940.4583333333334,
      "text": "a testament to the extent of knowledge networks in the early modern period."
    }
  ],
